environment changes do not persist.\n\n\n## Original user question (solution sections removed)\nTitle: How to compose a larger circuit onto a smaller circuit in Qiskit, adding extra quantum registers to some fixed list\n\n$\\newcommand{\\ket}[1]{|{#1}\\rangle}$ I have a Qiskit quantum circuit for multiplication $M$, that I want to onto another qiskit quantum circuit for inner product of vectors $I$. $M$ uses some ancillary qubits to perform the map $\\ket{a}\\ket{b}\\ket{c} \\mapsto \\ket{a}\\ket{b}\\ket{c\\oplus (a\\cdot b)}$. Ideally when I compose $M$ onto $I$ as a subroutine, I would only want to declare and map the quantum states (registers) of $I$ that I care about: $\\ket{a},\\ket{b},\\ket{c}$ and let the additional qubits be counted as some ancillas in $I$, maybe in some fixed register , whose length can be appropriately extended. According to the Qiskit documentation, to , $M$ can be narrower or of equal width to $I$, and methods like have been deprecated and removed. How can I go about doing this, if at all? I would be okay using any other SDK like Cirq, as I need a QASM2 at the end. But my circuits use classically controlled operations, which Cirq does not seem to support.\n\n```\ncompose\n```\n\n```\nanc\n```\n\n```\ncompose\n```\n\n```\nextend\n```\n\n```\ndump\n```\n\nOnly inspect and edit this task workspace and installed dependency APIs. Reference fixes, evaluation tests, other cases and previous repairs are unavailable inputs."
